  "conclusion": "All three full-schedule runs pass the original full ring gate at 6,400 updates: K3P has 66 terminal passing observations, KA2 47 and R1/R2 exactly 5 (required minimum 5). Matched saved 400-horizon baselines have terminal streaks 0/0/3 respectively and all fail. Final covariance errors change K3P 1.599982 to 0.567272, KA2 1.783269 to 0.563027 and R1/R2 0.525637 to 0.423491 (limit 0.85). All endpoints recover 16 modes with mass TV below 0.15 and HQ above 0.85. This supports schedule exhaustion as a major contributor to the ring failures; the coupled intervention does not identify LR alone as the cause. R1/R2 is fragile: update 6,317 fails minimum eigenvalue spread, and its endpoint ratio 0.157181 barely clears 0.15. KA2 last fails minimum spread at 5,617; K3P last fails covariance at 5,300. Every new arm still fails at the sampled 1,600/3,200/4,800 budget cuts. Frozen archived source/runtime, initial tensors/prior, actual target batches, cadence, clean-live sampling and the serialization boundary at 1,600 match. The first 1,600 baseline batches are verified by frozen-law replay and endpoint RNG state; the subsequent 4,800 by the actual baseline trace digest. No baseline is retrained. Source-bound receipts, restored states, final optimizer counts and saved-sample gate recomputations pass. This ring-only diagnostic changes neither original qualification results nor the current leaderboard. The separate clock audit remains unresolved.",
  "next_action": "Conclude this bounded three-run round. Prioritize K3P and KA2 for a future declared full-budget schedule policy comparison across the required task suite before selecting any global recipe. Inspect saved R1/R2 per-component spread and covariance near its late failures before proposing a repair. Treat clock-free eligibility as a separate mechanism question: longer schedules retain explicit time dependence. No automatic additional training, seed study, qualification or default adoption.",
